*Your core argument (the thing that keeps getting brushed over)*
• You suspect the constraint is foundational, not creative: no unique mechanism, a generic VSL intro, weak differentiation in a saturated funding-ad market where prospects are over-exposed and competitors run parallel spend hopping each other's ads.
• The "sticking factor" is missing. You're pulling people off a high-stimulation platform and not earning the attention in the first seconds, so even the ones who click don't see you as different or stay.
• You think SCIO is running theory top-down (overseer) instead of bottom-up (in the trenches), and skipping core fundamentals they teach in the DFY (done-for-you) program: unique mechanism, timeline, CTA, attention hook, retention.
• Two specific levers they keep ignoring: competitor analysis (raised multiple times) and channel cost (you know operators paying $7 to $12 per qualified DM, a much cheaper path than $300 booked calls).
*Possible wrong assumption to flag*
• ICP (ideal customer profile): medical practice owners were SCIO's call as a "good client," but they're sophisticated and hard. Your instinct says W2 professionals and general business owners are easier and closer to your actual experience. The offer itself isn't validated yet, so you may be stress-testing creative against the wrong audience.
*The meta-tension*
• You're trying to stay humble and suspend your intuition to honor their process, but you don't want to keep suspending it only to keep confirming what your gut already flagged. You want calculated and precise, not "throw mud at the wall."
*Questions to put to SCIO (force commitment, not vibes)*
